# Karpathy lightweight coding rules

## 1. Language

- Respond in Chinese by default and write project documentation primarily in Chinese.
- Keep code identifiers, commands, paths, and established technical terms in their conventional form.

## 2. Think before acting

- Define the goal, constraints, impact, and verification before making changes.
- Present a brief plan of 3–6 items before actionable work.
- Surface competing interpretations instead of choosing silently.
- Wait for confirmation before deletion, overwrite, irreversible action, or a major tradeoff.

## 3. Keep changes simple and surgical

- Implement only what the user requested. Do not add speculative features.
- Avoid abstractions for one-off logic and do not refactor unrelated code.
- Match existing style and remove only unused content introduced by the current change.
- Make every changed line trace to the request, a required test, or a direct documentation update.

## 4. Document large changes

Treat work spanning three or more logical modules, or changing architecture, public APIs, databases, authentication, authorization, build, deployment, migration, or rollback as a large change. Also treat explicit requests for design or implementation plans as large changes.

Create and approve before coding:

- `docs/plans/YYYY-MM-DD-主题-design.md`
- `docs/plans/YYYY-MM-DD-主题-implementation.md`

Use the templates in `docs/plans/`. Do not create formal planning documents for ordinary changes.

## 5. Implement first, then test in one batch

- Add or update unit tests for every new or changed testable behavior.
- Finish all code for the current task before writing or updating tests.
- Do not run tests after every class, function, file, or individual test.
- After code and tests are complete, run affected-module tests and then the complete project test suite.
- Finish with applicable type checks, lint, and build.
- During failure repair, run focused tests as needed; after the fix, run the complete suite again.
- Never delete tests, weaken assertions, or skip tests merely to make them pass.
- Do not use TDD unless the user explicitly requests test-first development.

Documentation, comments, formatting, static assets, and behavior-neutral configuration changes do not require new unit tests. When the complete suite cannot run, explain why and state the unverified scope.

## 6. Complete and report

- Report actual changes, test commands, real results, and remaining risks.
- For large changes, update the implementation document only at phase boundaries, not after each file.
- State anything not verified.
